  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/b958bd4db4353cfe7a852b79f7144d0b48b85c48b3d97f813b09db2858ec110a.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?",
  "worked_solution": "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-3,y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-3,0,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/666ae97e41e825161c68cc6146a06c70b87c6d84b11ae8116c0d415c340987fa.png)",
